{"conversation_id": "conv_001", "status": "started", "response": "Imagine two tiny particles, like electrons or photons, that are created together in a special way. They become “entangled,” which means their properties are linked, no matter how far apart they go.

For example, each particle can spin in different ways, but until you measure them, their spins aren’t decided yet. When you finally check one particle, its spin becomes definite—and at that exact moment, the other particle’s spin is also fixed, in a matching way, even if it’s on the other side of the galaxy.

Nothing is traveling faster than light; instead, the pair shares one combined quantum state. Entanglement is this weird link that only shows up in the tiny quantum world, and it’s used in things like quantum computers and quantum cryptography.", "tokens_used": {"input": 3865, "output": 164, "cached": 0}}
